index. Space in the first column = index matches HEAD. D in the second column = file was deleted in the working tree. So the marker is committed in HEAD, but someone deleted it from the working tree.\n\nHmm, so actually the marker IS committed. The only issue is it's deleted from the working tree. So `ls .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` would fail right now.\n\nSo \"what's still incomplete\": the marker file needs to exist in the working tree (the Phase 5 step 2 says \"Verify the marker: ls .story-updates/STORY_ID.passed\"). It's deleted from the working tree right now.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Recreate the marker file (touch)\n2. Verify it exists\n3. Since the marker is already in HEAD and I just recreate it (identical content - empty file), `git add` + `git commit` — well, if the content is identical to HEAD, git won't have anything to commit. Actually the marker is an empty file. Recreating it identically would make the working tree match HEAD again, so there'd be nothing to commit.\n\nWait, but then \"git commit -m 'chore: mark STORY_ID as passed'\" would fail with \"nothing to commit\". Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually, the marker file is tracked in HEAD. If I just `touch` it (recreate the empty file), the working tree matches HEAD, git status becomes clean for that file, and there's nothing to commit. That's fine — the marker exists, which is the key requirement.\n\nBut wait, why is the marker deleted from the working tree? That's strange. Let me think... Maybe a previous process deleted it. Or maybe it's part of the ralph harness flow.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest interpretation is: the story is essentially complete. The marker is already committed. I just need to restore the marker in the working tree and ensure everything is in order.\n\nLet me focus on what the actual deliverables are:\n1. ✅ package.json exists with correct content (committed, tests pass)\n2. ✅ Tests pass (4/4)\n3. Need to restore marker file in working tree\n4. Commit + push (though may be nothing to commit)\n\nLet me verify the marker is actually in HEAD and committed, and check what the commit is.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. I'm overthinking the git state. Let me just:\n1. Verify package.json is committed (check git log for it, git show)\n2. Recreate the marker\n3. Follow the orchestrator loop: spawn planner, spawn code-reviewer\n4. Create marker, commit, push\n\nBut I want to be careful about the \"do not redo committed work\" instruction. The code is committed. So I should:\n- Delegate the \"plan\" (planner) — a brief plan confirming what's left\n- Delegate the \"review\" (code-reviewer) — review the committed package.json + test\n- Do Phase 5 myself (marker + commit + push)\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should even be spawning the planner and code-reviewer. The high-priority instruction is very clear: \"YOU ARE THE ORCHESTRATOR — SPAWN A SUB-AGENT FOR EVERY TASK. Do NOT write or edit source code (or tests) yourself. For EVERY task — plan, code, review, fix — spawn a sub-agent and delegate the work to it.\"\n\nAnd the loop contract: \"MUST spawn and run planner + code-reviewer every loop — do not skip delegation and do the work inline.\"\n\nSo yes, I should spawn the planner and code-reviewer. The \"code\" part is already done (committed), so I don't need to spawn a coder — but I should verify the code is actually complete and committed first. If there's something to implement, I'd spawn a coder.\n\nLet me check: is there anything to implement? The package.json is committed. The test is committed and passing. So no, nothing to implement. The code work is done.\n\nSo my plan:\n1. Verify state (done — package.json committed, tests pass, marker deleted from worktree but in HEAD)\n2. Spawn planner to produce a plan for closing the story (confirming what's left: marker restore, commit, push) — actually the planner is read-only (tools: Read, Grep, Glob). It can't run bash. So the planner would just plan.\n3. Spawn code-reviewer to review the committed changes (tools include Bash so it can run git commands)\n4. After review passes, do Phase 5: restore marker, commit, push\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety. Let me re-examine. The code-reviewer needs to review the diff. But the code is already committed (no uncommitted diff). So the reviewer would review recent commits. That's fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Since everything is committed, maybe the cleanest path is:\n1. Spawn code-reviewer to review the package.json + test that's committed (this is the \"review\" phase)\n2. If it passes, I do Phase 5 (marker + commit + push)\n\nAnd the planner... the loop contract says I must run planner + code-reviewer every loop. But the story is essentially done. Let me be practical and run both since the contract requires it, but keep them focused.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Is spawning a planner even useful here? The work is done. A planner would just say \"the work is done, here's what's left: restore marker, commit, push\". That's not super valuable, but the contract requires it.\n\nLet me think about efficiency vs. contract compliance. The contract is quite emphatic. But I also don't want to waste tokens on a pointless planner run.\n\nLet me make a decision: I'll spawn the code-reviewer (essential, and it can actually verify the committed work). For the planner, given the work is already done and committed, I'll spawn it too since the contract says \"MUST spawn and run planner + code-reviewer every loop\". But I'll make the planner's task focused: confirm the implementation is complete against the 3 ACs and produce a short plan for the remaining closeout steps.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider once more. The planner has tools Read/Grep/Glob only (no Bash). So it can read package.json and test file, but can't run git or node. So it can verify the content of package.json against the ACs by reading it. That's useful.\n\nLet me spawn both the planner and code-reviewer in parallel (they're independent — planner plans, reviewer reviews). Actually, they can run concurrently since they don't depend on each other.\n\nWait, but the loop is: plan → code → review → fix. So planner first, then code, then review. But code is already done. So it's plan → (code already done) → review. I can run planner and reviewer in parallel since code is done and review doesn't depend on planner's output.\n\nHmm, but conceptually the planner's output would inform... no, the planner here is just confirming state. Let me run them in parallel to be efficient.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to run them in parallel or sequentially. The instruction says \"Wait for every child and read each result before continuing.\" and \"When you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\"\n\nSince the planner and reviewer are independent (both read-only analysis of the already-committed code), I can run them in parallel. Let me do that.\n\nLet me craft the prompts:\n\n**Planner prompt**: Include the full ecc-planner.md content + task. The task: STORY-setup-001a, the 3 ACs, the current state (package.json content, tests passing, committed), and ask it to confirm the implementation is complete against the ACs and produce a plan for the remaining closeout (marker restore, commit, push). Read-only.\n\n**Code-reviewer prompt**: Include the full ecc-code-reviewer.md content + task. The task: review the committed package.json and test/package.test.cjs for STORY-setup-001a against the 3 ACs. Check for quality issues. It can use Bash to run git commands and node tests.\n\nLet me get the exact agent file contents. I have them from the Read above. Let me extract the \"prompt\" portion (after the frontmatter).\n\nFor the planner, the content after frontmatter starts at line 7 \"## Prompt Defense Baseline\" through line 222.\n\nFor the reviewer, the content after frontmatter starts at line 7 \"## P